WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.040
As a developer I quickly got into the habit&nbsp;
of pushing to GitHub multiple times a day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.320
So, it surprises me when I hear that Operations&nbsp;
teams are tackling their infrastructure automation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.840
with a lot of manual processes. Hey everyone, my&nbsp;
name is Sai Vennam, and today I want to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:22.080
Ansible. Ansible is an open source infrastructure&nbsp;
automation tool, initially developed by Red Hat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.200
and enables you to tackle kind of all of the&nbsp;
major challenges that come with infrastructure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.880
It enables you to do so with code, or&nbsp;
in other terms infrastructure as code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.880
Now Ansible has three major kind of uses and I'll&nbsp;
start with the first one because this is generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.080
the one that people tend to gravitate to when&nbsp;
they hear infrastructure automation, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.600
the provisioning use case. So the first step of&nbsp;
kind of any infrastructure story, you've got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.280
have some infrastructure in the first place. So,&nbsp;
let's say that using Ansible we've already spun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:04.000
up some environment, let's say it's a VPC and my&nbsp;
favorite cloud provider, and within this VPC I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:15.360
got three virtual machines. So, we'll say we've&nbsp;
got one, two, and then a third one as well. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.400
these set of virtual machines are going to&nbsp;
essentially be our set of hosts. In the Ansible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.360
world, we're going to call this an inventory. So,&nbsp;
it's basically the set of hosts that Ansible can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:35.280
work against. So, what we're going to call this&nbsp;
is an inventory. Now within this inventory we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:41.520
actually create even more sub categorizations and&nbsp;
Ansible calls this patterns. So, let's say these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.600
virtual machines have different, you know,&nbsp;
underlying tasks and use cases and services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.200
that they're going to be running. So, these&nbsp;
first two virtual machines will call them web&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:54.640
because they're going to be used for&nbsp;
web servers, and then this last one

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.720
we're going to call this DB because we're going&nbsp;
to run MySql database on it. So, there we go we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:07.760
got our infrastructure we've got that provision&nbsp;
kind of step already done, and I want to jump to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:13.600
the second thing that Ansible is really good at&nbsp;
it's config management. And actually I'd say this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:19.200
is kind of the main use case that most people&nbsp;
think about when they first start to use Ansible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:25.040
and this kind of refers to the ability to&nbsp;
configure your actual infrastructure. So a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.240
simple example would be something like operating&nbsp;
system patches, or maybe making sure a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.160
service is running, maybe installing some service&nbsp;
onto a virtual machine. So in our use case today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.880
let's focus on that, let's focus on how we can&nbsp;
do config management with these virtual machines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:48.880
Now one of the key tenets of Ansible is that&nbsp;
it's declarative. Now out there there's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.480
the notion that Ansible can be both declarative as&nbsp;
well as procedural or imperative, and the idea is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.920
that, you know with Ansible you declare what you&nbsp;
want instead of focusing on how it gets there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.600
But in essence it can also be considered&nbsp;
procedural because the underlying tasks that run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.760
are procedural in nature, but as a user of Ansible&nbsp;
you're taking advantage of the declarative aspect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.760
of it meaning you define what you want rather&nbsp;
than how to get to whatever it is that you need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.200
Now today, what we want is to be able to run some&nbsp;
tasks against those VMs. Let's say we want to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.560
some OS patches and then install a programming&nbsp;
language, and then install MySQL. So, what we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:37.360
need is a playbook. So that's what we'll start&nbsp;
with first, we'll create an Ansible playbook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:44.320
and as the name indicates a playbook is going to&nbsp;
be a book of tasks or a set of of plays rather.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:51.280
Now our first play we want to run this against all&nbsp;
of our hosts and we want to just run a security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.440
patch, we want to make sure they're all running&nbsp;
the same version of the same operating system. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.440
that will be our first play. So a play is going&nbsp;
to be kind of consisting of three main things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.600
you need a name for that play, you need the host&nbsp;
that it's going to run against, and you need the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:14.080
actual tasks that will run. Now in this particular&nbsp;
play, let's say that we've got the hosts set to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.280
all because we want to run this against&nbsp;
all the hosts. And as far as the tasks

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.240
we'll define some set of tasks like&nbsp;
security patching and that kind of thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.240
Now key thing that I want to mention here, Ansible&nbsp;
takes takes advantage of YAML to do its kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.600
declarative nature of being able to declare what&nbsp;
you want. So this would this is obviously not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.720
a valid YAML but in the real world you know when&nbsp;
working with Ansible this would be in YAML format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.400
So that'll be our first play running&nbsp;
against all of the hosts in our inventory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:56.080
Now let's let's create some more plays here. Let's&nbsp;
create a play that runs directly against the hosts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:04.720
for our web servers. So we call that web,&nbsp;
right. And this is going to have a set of tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.640
maybe we need to do something like install Go and&nbsp;
install Python, maybe install some other services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:19.280
that are specific to our web servers. And finally&nbsp;
we'll create one more play in this playbook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:29.760
and for this one we'll set the host to be our&nbsp;
DB, and then for instead of tasks let's talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.480
about something called roles. Ansible has a&nbsp;
concept of roles which basically enable you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.440
group together actions that you run multiple&nbsp;
times. So instead of having to define all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:46.960
the tasks every single time you can just define&nbsp;
the roles and that will take advantage of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:52.720
know the underlying modules and tasks that need&nbsp;
to be run. By the way, a playbook is one way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.600
grouping together a set of tasks that you need&nbsp;
to run, it's a declarative way of doing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.360
but if you really want to keep things&nbsp;
procedural imperative you can actually run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:08.000
one-off tasks by themselves and do so in a in an&nbsp;
imperative way or a procedural way, but this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:13.920
kind of the way you would do a fully declarative&nbsp;
approach to automate a set of tasks against you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:20.880
know the set of hosts in your inventory. So, this&nbsp;
is our playbook and we want to execute this now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:28.240
That's going to bring me to my second tenet of&nbsp;
Ansible and that's the fact that it's agent-less.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:37.440
Now Ansible is agent-less in in the sense that&nbsp;
like a lot of other infrastructure automation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.880
tools, unlike a lot of other infrastructure&nbsp;
automations, you don't actually need to install an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:48.160
agent on the VMs that were provisioned. So,&nbsp;
Ansible is able to take advantage of SSH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.560
to actually directly go into those VMs and&nbsp;
run the tasks that it needs to. So, let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:59.360
see what that looks like right. So we take our&nbsp;
playbook and as a user we'll execute Ansible, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:05.360
Ansible can execute you know and most unix-like&nbsp;
environments, so we'll execute our playbook and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:12.000
it'll run against our virtual machines and there&nbsp;
we go, we're happy. But here's one thing to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.240
about. Now chances are we wouldn't be running&nbsp;
Ansible on our local machine, right. At the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:21.120
end of the day it has to store SSH keys and a&nbsp;
lot of kind of other secure configuration and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.680
you're not really trying to run automation off&nbsp;
your local machine. So there's a capability out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:32.640
there called AWX, or Ansible tower, which is&nbsp;
the downstream project of the open source AWX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:41.040
and that's basically a graphical interface and&nbsp;
set of APIs that enable you to run Ansible and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.800
operationalize it across your organization. So&nbsp;
whether you're using Ansible, or Ansible Tower, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.160
user will come in execute that playbook and there&nbsp;
we go, we'll have our config management executed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:59.520
Now the third aspect that I want to talk about&nbsp;
with Ansible is the fact that it's idempotent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:05.520
Now what exactly does that mean? So idempotent refers to an operation that can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:12.640
run multiple times without changing beyond the&nbsp;
initial application. In other terms, the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:16.960
time I run this operation it should recognize&nbsp;
that the security patches need to be installed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.960
the services need to be installed, the Python and&nbsp;
Go and MySQL, or whatever needs to be installed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.680
has to be done. The second time I execute that&nbsp;
same playbook it's going to recognize it's already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:33.040
done it and it's going to say hey nothing needs&nbsp;
to be done. But let's say I come back my second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:40.720
day and I say oh we need to actually update the&nbsp;
operating system version from you know 1.0 to 1.1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.280
and we run this same playbook&nbsp;
and Ansible, or Ansible Tower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:49.120
and then recognize it and says hey that operating&nbsp;
system has changed but nothing else did it's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:55.840
to execute the only action needs to to kind of&nbsp;
resolve that difference. Now one thing to consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:01.360
all of these tasks have underlying modules that&nbsp;
power them and you want to make sure make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:05.840
that the modules themselves are programmed in&nbsp;
a way that's idempotent. And this is important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:10.720
and it kind of brings me to my last point&nbsp;
here is that Ansible is community driven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.840
That means that if I'm trying to do these things&nbsp;
in a playbook that I kind of outline today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.720
chances are someone out there has already&nbsp;
developed the playbooks, the modules, the tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:27.120
that I need to actually do those operations. Those&nbsp;
are basically published as collections in what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:34.000
called Ansible Galaxy, which is a repository where&nbsp;
developers can kind of contribute their Ansible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:39.200
kind of modules, or playbooks, or tasks whatever&nbsp;
they might have created in the ecosystem. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.600
one of the great things about Ansible is that it's&nbsp;
so community driven that chances are whatever I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.960
need to do I'll be able to find in that galaxy,&nbsp;
Ansible Galaxy. The one kind of disclaimer I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.600
say, make sure run these operations locally,&nbsp;
make sure they're item potent, make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:59.200
that you know executing them multiple times&nbsp;
responds in a way that's kind of predictable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:05.440
So all that being said, we've kind of talked&nbsp;
about how Ansible is a declarative language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:10.000
it doesn't require an agent to be able to&nbsp;
execute in these kind of end environments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.280
in these virtual machines. It's idempotent based&nbsp;
on the modules that you're using to enable you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:20.960
avoid things like config drift by making sure&nbsp;
that any changes that you make should be done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.360
in your infrastructure automation story,&nbsp;
whether it's playbooks, or roles, or modules.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.000
And then finally, you know you can rest easy&nbsp;
knowing that chances are whatever you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.560
automate is available in the community. Now&nbsp;
the third use case I mentioned, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:39.360
three things that you can do with Ansible, it's&nbsp;
going to be app deployment. Now I'm not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:44.720
go into too much detail here today about the app&nbsp;
deployment use case, but the fact is you can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:50.720
Ansible to then take it a step further and deploy&nbsp;
your actual web applications and workloads into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:56.720
the virtual machines, it's also a capability&nbsp;
available by Ansible. I hope this was helpful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:00.720
If you like this video or have any comments,&nbsp;
be sure to drop a like or comment below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:04.800
Stay tuned and follow us for more videos&nbsp;
like this in the future. Thank you.

